1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A barrier holds every process at one point until the last one arrives.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The arrival gate holds early arrivals; the counter counts them.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The departure gate resets the counter so the barrier can be used again.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
A barrier is a counter plus a release condition; reuse needs a way to tell one round from the next.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
GATE CSE 2006 gives three processes and a binary semaphore S initialised to one.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Lines 1 to 3 are the arrival gate, and they are correct.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Line 4 spins on the counter outside the critical section, and that is the flaw.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Lines 5 to 11 are the departure gate: the last one out resets both counters.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The search visits every interleaving of the three processes, not one convenient trace.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
With one invocation there are no deadlocks and no early releases.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The counter climbs to three and stops, because only three processes exist.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So the first invocation is correct — and the exam question is about the second.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
With two invocations the search finds deadlocked end states.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
One process reads three, leaves the loop, and re-enters before the others read.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Its second arrival pushes the counter to four, and four never equals three.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Measured: eight deadlocked end states, and the counter reaches 2n minus one.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Option A replaces the departure block with a single decrement, and it fails.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Option B puts a gate at the start, and the counter can no longer pass n.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Option C disables context switching, so the spin can never be satisfied.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Option D makes the departure counter private, so it is never reset.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Option B bounds the counter at n, which is what the first question asks for.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
But a full search still finds a stalled second invocation under B.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Round two stalls because only a completed round two can clear the gate.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The exam answer and the reusable barrier are two different questions.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The naive barrier spins until the counter returns to n.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
It finishes some runs and deadlocks in others — which is the worst kind of wrong.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
A fast process decrements to two while a slow one spins for three.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Repair: give each round a different value, so a stale read cannot match.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Each process flips its own sense, then waits for the shared one to match.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
A stale read cannot match, because the value changes meaning every round.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Measured over two invocations: zero deadlocks and zero early releases.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Three rounds, three alternating values: true, false, true.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The two-phase barrier counts up on arrival and down on departure.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The invariant is that the counter equals the processes still inside.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Measured: no deadlocks, and the non-atomic counter changes nothing.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Three barriers, three answers to "how do I know which round this is".

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
The counts: n minus one spin, and the counter can reach 2n minus one.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Reading procedure: find the counter, find who resets it, then try two rounds.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Three traps: one trace proves nothing, spinning is not blocked, arrivals are not enough.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
A barrier needs an arrival gate and a departure gate, and the departure gate must tell one round from the next.

